    "answer_payload": "B",
    "locked": true,
    "locked_at": "2026-10-20T16:12:05.022Z",
    "request_id": "8b09ee5f-f989-4c62-b5b8-024efd1420a3",
    "answer_hash": "df7e70e5021544f4834bbee64a9e3789febc4be81470df629cad6ddb03320a5c",
    "evaluation_status": "EVALUATED",
    "created_at": "2026-10-20T16:12:05.022Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-20T16:12:05.022Z"
  },
  {
    "id": "1e5a302b-312b-4092-bc9a-bce716b310d3",
    "attempt_id": "059a1135-f1a5-4d39-9e43-fb09ca546883",
    "question_id": "e1e79c71-ebf5-4c9c-ac5b-73b99fc54cbe",
    "answer_type": "TEXT",
    "answer_payload": "The CSS Containment API (contain property) improves browser rendering performance by isolating a subtree of the DOM, allowing the browser to skip unnecessary layout, style, and paint recalculations for the rest of the page.\nDifferences between values:\nlayout: Ensures that the internal layout of the contained element is completely isolated from the rest of the page. Changes inside do not trigger layout recalculations outside, and vice versa.\npaint: Ensures that the descendants of the element are not painted outside its bounds. This prevents descendant overflows from triggering repaints on outer elements, and hides the element entirely if it is off-screen.\nstrict: Turns on all containment rules (size, layout, style, and paint) for the element, treating it as an isolated box for maximum performance optimization.",
    "locked": true,
    "locked_at": "2026-10-20T16:13:52.279Z",
    "request_id": "673f8894-520e-46c7-88ec-505439de1709",
    "answer_hash": "d20eb5940d5063c8a42592442515709b2e282fb895c0bc19450250f10f9bb981",
    "evaluation_status": "EVALUATED",
    "created_at": "2026-10-20T16:13:52.279Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-20T16:13:52.279Z"
  },
  {
    "id": "de388b70-33b1-426e-8ca7-229f4d287a5f",
    "attempt_id": "059a1135-f1a5-4d39-9e43-fb09ca546883",
    "question_id": "97fb0490-5897-43f3-a798-d19890acf9b7",
    "answer_type": "TEXT",
    "answer_payload": "CSS subgrid (<code style=\"background:rgba(128,128,128,0.2);padding:2px 4px;border-radius:3px;\">grid-template-columns: subgrid</code> or <code style=\"background:rgba(128,128,128,0.2);padding:2px 4px;border-radius:3px;\">grid-template-rows: subgrid</code>) allows a nested grid item to adopt the track sizes defined by its parent grid in a specific dimension. Instead of defining its own tracks, the nested element uses the parent's track lines, enabling perfect alignment of nested items across different parent grid cells.\n\nPrimary Limitations:\n1. Dimension limitation: Subgrid can only be applied in a single dimension at a time (you cannot subgrid both rows and columns simultaneously on the same element, though you can use subgrid for one and explicit tracks for the other).\n2. Track sizing restriction: Subgrid items cannot use intrinsic sizing keywords like <code style=\"background:rgba(128,128,128,0.2);padding:2px 4px;border-radius:3px;\">min-content</code> or <code style=\"background:rgba(128,128,128,0.2);padding:2px 4px;border-radius:3px;\">max-content</code> on the subgrid tracks themselves; they inherit sizes from the parent.\n3. Gap handling: While subgrids can define their own gaps, they cannot easily override the parent's track positioning within that specific subgrid track span.",
    "locked": true,
    "locked_at": "2026-10-20T16:14:31.964Z",
    "request_id": "1399d030-72f2-48a4-b3ea-0e738f557c99",
    "answer_hash": "6d674ee5e6a4bb35b98b52e54a6839d8b05c8b14484a0793a35704f231332e45",
    "evaluation_status": "EVALUATED",
    "created_at": "2026-10-20T16:14:31.964Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-20T16:14:31.964Z"
  },
